      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸ σὲ οἰκονομεῖν ἀναδέξασθαι χάριν οἶδα εἰληφώς, οὐ δεδωκώς",
          "english_quote": "Hence I know that, in undertaking to oversee you, I have received a favor, not given one.",
          "issue": "The draft makes Peter undertake the oversight of Clement. The stronger contextual and syntactic analysis takes σέ as the accusative subject of the infinitival expression: Clement undertakes to administer, and Peter regards that undertaking as a favor received. The preceding commission and the subsequent ἀνεδέξω support this connection. The nominative participles εἰληφώς and δεδωκώς establish Peter as the recipient rather than giver of the favor; they do not establish him as the subject of the embedded infinitives.",
          "suggested_revision": "Proposed replacement for fresh review: “Hence I know that, in your undertaking to administer, I have received a favor, not given one.” Explain the analysis of τὸ σὲ οἰκονομεῖν ἀναδέξασθαι as the nominalized undertaking, with χάριν expressing its characterization as a favor. Retain any defensible alternative as an interpretive alternative, not as an established reading.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τὸ σὲ οἰκονομεῖν ἀναδέξασθαι",
          "english_quote": "A further, less explicit analysis makes Clement the subject of ἀναδέξασθαι as well, so that Clement undertakes the administration, but the change of subject is not overtly marked.",
          "issue": "The source_issues disclosure misranks the alternatives and overlooks the overt accusative σέ as a possible subject of the entire articular infinitival expression. Clement's being the subject of ἀναδέξασθαι is not an analysis requiring an entirely unmarked subject change. Consequently, the existing disclosure does not adequately qualify the main text's assignment of oversight to Peter.",
          "suggested_revision": "Rewrite the grammatical note to distinguish the first-person subject of οἶδα and its nominative participles from the accusative subject of the infinitival expression. Present Clement's undertaking the administration as the leading analysis, while identifying the remaining interpretive alternatives without proposing a correction to the supplied Greek.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "17",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": []
    },
    {
      "reference": "18",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἵνα ἕκαστος, ἐπιδικαζόμενος τοῦ πάντας φίλους ἔχειν, αὐτὸν διαλλάττων σώζηται, τῆς αὐτοῦ ἐπακούων ὁμιλίας",
          "english_quote": "so that each person, pressing his claim to have everyone as friends, may be saved by reconciling him and heeding his discourse.",
          "issue": "The wording preserves the active construction with αὐτόν as its object and does not invent the other party to the reconciliation. Nevertheless, the identity of ἕκαστος, the referent of αὐτόν, and the resulting relationship between reconciliation and heeding the discourse remain interpretively uncertain. The source_issues notes disclose this sufficiently; acceptance of the wording does not settle those relationships or establish textual corruption.",
          "suggested_revision": "No replacement proposed. Retain both interpretive source_issues notes and their provisional status. Do not replace “him” or “his” with a named referent, change the construction to self-reconciliation, or remove the existing publication hold without a separate assessment of the referents and the usage of διαλλάττω.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "19",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "σὲ Ἰακώβῳ τῷ ἀδελφῷ τοῦ κυρίου διαπέμψαι ἐν ἐπιτομῇ ἀναγραψάμενον",
          "english_quote": "you not hesitate to send James, the Lord’s brother, a concise written account",
          "issue": "The rendering takes σέ as the resumed subject of διαπέμψαι and ἀναγραψάμενον and supplies the account as the understood thing sent. That is a defensible interpretation of the recording-and-sending instructions, but the extended construction and the scope of the final μὴ ὀκνήσῃς remain unsettled. The source_issues note adequately identifies these choices rather than presenting the supplied syntax as corrupt.",
          "suggested_revision": "No replacement proposed. Retain the syntactic disclosure and its existing publication hold. Acceptance of this English construction does not establish that the alternatives have been eliminated. Further assessment should concern the resumptive accusative and the relationships among the infinitives and μὴ ὀκνήσῃς, not an assumed transcription error.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὐκ ἀμαθὴς ἀνὴρ καὶ ζωοποιοὺς λόγους ἀγνοῶν καὶ ἐκκλησίας κανόνα. οὐκ εἰδὼς τὴν τοῦ διδάσκοντος ἐπιστεύθη καθέδραν",
          "english_quote": "after me it was not to an unlearned man—one ignorant of life-giving words and not knowing the church’s rule—that the teacher’s chair was entrusted.",
          "issue": "The main text adopts a broad scope for the first οὐκ while retaining the explicit negation in οὐκ εἰδώς. This yields a coherent interpretation without silently deleting either negation. The alternative local scope of the first negation and the interrupted printed punctuation remain genuine interpretive concerns, both disclosed in source_issues. The interpretation must not be treated as a correction to the Greek or as a securely established distribution of the negation.",
          "suggested_revision": "No replacement proposed. Retain the scope-of-negation note and the separate editorial-punctuation note. Do not silently redistribute the first οὐκ across the participles or remove the existing interpretive publication hold. Any subsequently revised rendering requires a fresh review of both negations.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "20",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
